{
  "title": "📏 Areas of Similar Triangles — The Square Rule",
  "hook": "If you double every side of a triangle, what happens to its area? It doesn't just double — it quadruples! This is the 'square rule' for similar figures.",
  "explanation": "<div style=\"background:#F3E5F5;border-left:4px solid #7B1FA2;border-radius:8px;padding:12px 16px;margin:12px 0\"><strong style=\"color:#7B1FA2\">Area Ratio Theorem:</strong> The ratio of areas of two similar triangles equals the square of the ratio of their corresponding sides.</div><div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#E3F2FD,#BBDEFB);border-left:4px solid #1565C0;border-radius:8px;padding:12px 16px;margin:12px 0;font-family:'Courier New',monospace;font-size:1.05rem;text-align:center\"><div style=\"font-size:0.7rem;text-transform:uppercase;font-weight:700;color:#1565C0;margin-bottom:4px\">Area Ratio</div><strong>Area(△ABC) / Area(△DEF) = (AB/DE)² = (BC/EF)² = (CA/FD)²</strong></div><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:12px 0;font-size:0.88rem\"><tr><th style=\"background:#E3F2FD;padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #BBDEFB;text-align:left;font-weight:700\">Side ratio</th><th style=\"background:#E3F2FD;padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #BBDEFB;text-align:left;font-weight:700\">Area ratio</th><th style=\"background:#E3F2FD;padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #BBDEFB;text-align:left;font-weight:700\">Example</th></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">1:1</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">1:1</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">Congruent (same size)</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">1:2</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">1:4</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">Double the sides → 4× the area</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">2:3</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">4:9</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">Sides in ratio 2:3 → areas in 4:9</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">3:5</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">9:25</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">Sides in ratio 3:5 → areas in 9:25</td></tr></table><div style=\"background:#FFF8E1;border-left:4px solid #FFB300;border-radius:8px;padding:10px 14px;margin:10px 0;font-size:0.88rem\">💡 <strong>Tip:</strong> This also works with altitudes, medians, and angle bisectors: area ratio = (altitude ratio)².</div>",
  "worked_example": "<b>Example:</b> △ABC ~ △PQR with AB = 8 cm, PQ = 12 cm. If Area(△ABC) = 32 cm², find Area(△PQR).<br><br><div style=\"margin:12px 0\"><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#4A90D9;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">1</div><div><strong>Side ratio</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">AB/PQ = 8/12 = 2/3</span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#4A90D9;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">2</div><div><strong>Area ratio</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">(2/3)² = 4/9</span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#4A90D9;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">3</div><div><strong>Set up equation</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">32 / Area(△PQR) = 4/9</span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#4A90D9;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">4</div><div><strong>Solve</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">Area(△PQR) = 32 × 9/4 = 72 cm²</span></div></div></div>",
  "try_this": {
    "question": "Two similar triangles have areas 100 cm² and 64 cm². If a side of the larger is 20 cm, find the corresponding side of the smaller.",
    "hint": "Area ratio = (side ratio)². So side ratio = √(area ratio).",
    "answer": "(side/20)² = 64/100 → side/20 = 8/10 = 4/5 → side = 16 cm."
  },
  "fun_fact": "This 'square rule' extends to all similar shapes: if you triple the radius of a circle, the area becomes 9 times larger!"
}